[1;38;2;25;103;210m  `//go:build !darwin` and returns `errNoRawMode`, leaving the terminal[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  untouched. Full-screen agent UIs therefore only work on macOS. The README[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  must state this explicitly (the plan only says "macOS" in passing under[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  prerequisites and never says what happens elsewhere).[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  [m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  3. **The verification step `git diff --stat *.go produces no output` cannot[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  pass at baseline — and this is a blocking defect in the plan.** Current repo[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  state before any work is already dirty:[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m     - `M main.go` — 13 insertions already present.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m     - `?? build.go`, `?? build_test.go` — untracked (so `git diff` never shows[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  them anyway).[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  [m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m     Running that command today prints `main.go | 13 +++`. The checklist item[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  is therefore unverifiable and would falsely implicate this task in[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  pre-existing changes. **Fix the verification to be baseline-relative**, e.g.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  record `git status --porcelain` (or `git stash create` / tree hash) before[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  the edit and assert that the only *new* path is `README.md`, and state[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  explicitly that the existing `main.go` diff and untracked `build*.go` are[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  pre-existing and out of scope.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  [m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  ## Gaps — behaviors the plan should document or consciously exclude[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  [m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **Exit codes are undocumented.** `run`: `2` for usage/unknown agent/no[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  agents/pass-through-with-multiple-agents, `127` for a missing binary, `1` on[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  PTY/runtime error, otherwise the agent's own code is propagated. `build`: `2`[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  on arg errors, `127` on missing binary, `1` on stage/verification failure,[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  `0` on success. `orch` with no args prints usage to **stderr** and exits `2`.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  Either add a short table or say explicitly these are out of scope.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **`orch build` is hard-wired** to `buildPlanAgent = agy` and[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  `buildReviewAgent = command-code`. Nothing in the plan says the other agents[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  are unusable with `build` — the README should.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **Artifact naming is numbered, not a flat glob.** Actual names:[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  `1-plan.md`, `2-plan-review.md`, `3-implement.md` (cycle 1) or `3-fix.md`[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  (later cycles), `4-review.md`, per-stage `<name>.log`, and `verify.log`. The[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  plan's `*-plan.md` / `*-review.md` sketch is loose; show a real example[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  directory.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **`build` limits/limits-facing knobs unstated:** at most `buildPlanCycles =[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  3` implementation/review cycles, then failure; prior-artifact context is[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  truncated to `buildContextLimit = 8000` bytes with the full file always[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  referenced by path. The cycle cap is worth a sentence; the truncation limit[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  is not README material.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **Pass-through is validated, not "untouched."** The plan's own wording is[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  accurate ("permission-bypass flags strictly blocked") but the README's tone[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  should match `usage()` — orch *adds* no approval-skipping flags and *refuses*[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  to forward those supplied after `--`; avoid implying orch sanitizes the[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  agent's internal behavior.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **Agent descriptions:** use the registry/usage strings verbatim[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  (`Antigravity CLI (native terminal UI)`, `Command Code coding agent`, `GitHub[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  Copilot CLI`). The plan's "**Google** Antigravity CLI" is an added[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  attribution not present in the code — either drop it or verify it; drift[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  between README and `usage()` is the failure mode to avoid.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **Flag parsing position-independence** (`orch run agy -c` and `orch run -c[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  agy` both work; unknown flag → exit 2 with message) is undocumented. One line[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  suffices.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **Duplication risk:** `usage()` already carries a full flag/agent summary.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  The README should either mirror it exactly or defer to it. The plan does not[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  address keeping the two in sync — worth an explicit note that `usage()` is[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  the source of truth.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  [m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  ## Risks[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  [m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **Low overall risk.** No Go files are modified; worst case is documentation[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  drift.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **Main risk is drift from `usage()` and `go.mod`** — already materialized[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  in the plan's Go version and platform claims.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **Step 4 (`go vet` / `go test`) is harmless but should not be used to judge[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  this change**: tests are unaffected by a README, so a failure there is[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  pre-existing and must not be attributed to this task (and must not trigger a[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  "fix" of Go files, which the task forbids).[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  - **`README.md` precedence:** confirm there is no existing README (there is[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  not) and no docs/ directory convention to follow.[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  [m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  ## Recommended changes to the plan[m[K
[1;38;2;25;103;210m  [m[K
